Sealed Secrets implementation for the MySQL secret (cvs-2)

Run kubectl config current-context before each step. Steps 1 to 6 and 10 run on the service cluster, step 9 on the management cluster.

1. Install the controller (once per cluster)

helm repo add sealed-secrets https://bitnami.github.io/sealed-secrets
helm repo update
helm install sealed-secrets sealed-secrets/sealed-secrets -n kube-system
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep sealed
kubectl get crd sealedsecrets.bitnami.com

2. Install the CLI

brew install kubeseal

3. Back up the existing Secret (keep it out of Git)

kubectl get secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 -o yaml > ~/secure-backups/backup-secret.yaml
kubectl get secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 -o jsonpath='{.data}' | shasum   # note for step 10

4. Read the real passwords

kubectl get secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 -o jsonpath='{.data.MYSQL_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d; echo
kubectl get secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 -o jsonpath='{.data.MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d; echo

5. Create the plain secret locally and seal it (in /tmp, not in the repo)

cd /tmp
kubectl create secret generic cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 \
  --from-literal=username='cvs' \
  --from-literal=password='<app password>' \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_DATABASE='cvs' \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_USER='cvs' \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_PASSWORD='<app password>' \
  --from-literal=MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD='<root password>' \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > secret.yaml

kubeseal --controller-name sealed-secrets --controller-namespace kube-system \
  --format yaml < secret.yaml > sealed-cvs-2.yaml
rm secret.yaml

--dry-run=client creates nothing in the cluster. The name and namespace must match exactly, because the sealed value only decrypts for them.

6. Turn the sealed output into a values file

cd <repo>/cvs
{
  echo "mysql:"
  echo "  sealed:"
  echo "    encryptedData:"
  sed -n '/^  encryptedData:/,/^  template:/p' /tmp/sealed-cvs-2.yaml | sed '1d;$d' | sed 's/^/  /'
} > values-cvs-2.yaml
grep -c ": Ag" values-cvs-2.yaml     # expect 6

With yq installed: yq '{"mysql":{"sealed":{"encryptedData": .spec.encryptedData}}}' /tmp/sealed-cvs-2.yaml > values-cvs-2.yaml

7. Change the chart (cvs/charts/mysql)

values.yaml: remove the plain username, password and rootPassword and add:

sealed:
  encryptedData: {}

templates/secret.yaml: replace the old Secret template with:

{{- if not .Values.sealed.encryptedData }}
{{- fail "mysql.sealed.encryptedData is empty: set it in the environment values file" }}
{{- end }}
apiVersion: bitnami.com/v1alpha1
kind: SealedSecret
metadata:
  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-mysql-secret
  namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
spec:
  encryptedData:
    {{- toYaml .Values.sealed.encryptedData | nindent 4 }}
  template:
    metadata:
      name: {{ .Release.Name }}-mysql-secret
      labels:
        {{- include "mysql.labels" . | nindent 8 }}
    type: Opaque

Move any backup or *_bkp* files out of templates/, because Helm renders every file there.

8. Test locally

cd <repo>/cvs
helm dependency update      # only if Chart.lock is out of sync
helm lint .
helm template cvs-2 . --namespace cvs-2 -f values-cvs-2.yaml \
  --set es.version=7.17.15-icu \
  --set frontend.image.repository=ghcr.io/cessda/cvs-frontend \
  --set frontend.image.tag=main-30 \
  --set es.imageRepository=ghcr.io/cessda/elasticsearch | grep -A12 "kind: SealedSecret"

The output should show six encrypted keys and no plain passwords.

9. Let the controller take over the existing Secret, then update ArgoCD

kubectl annotate secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 sealedsecrets.bitnami.com/managed="true"

Add values-cvs-2.yaml to cvs-2-app.yaml under spec.source.helm:

      valueFiles:
        - values-cvs-2.yaml

Commit and push the chart changes and values-cvs-2.yaml, then apply the Application on the management cluster:

git add cvs/Chart.lock cvs/charts/mysql cvs/values-cvs-2.yaml
git commit -m "mysql: replace plain Secret with SealedSecret for cvs-2"
git push
kubectl apply -f cvs-2-app.yaml -n argocd

Then sync cvs-2 in ArgoCD (hard refresh first if needed).

10. Verify (service cluster)

kubectl describe sealedsecret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2      # "Unsealed" event
kubectl get secret cvs-2-mysql-secret -n cvs-2 -o jsonpath='{.data}' | shasum   # equals step 3
kubectl get pods -n cvs-2

Notes for the document

	•	Every environment needs its own sealed values (values-staging.yaml, values-prod.yaml), sealed for its own secret name and namespace.
	•	Back up the controller's key, or the sealed files in Git can't be decrypted after a cluster rebuild: kubectl get secret -n kube-system -l sealedsecrets.bitnami.com/sealed-secrets-key -o yaml > ~/secure-backups/sealed-secrets-key.yaml
	•	To rotate a password, repeat steps 4 to 6 with the new value, commit, sync, and restart the pods.
